## 2. Software Architecture

**Scope.** This section focuses on how the software is organized from a logical point of view. Specifically, it is about how the software is structured to do it — which components exist, what each one is responsible for, and how they interact with each other.

We are not yet asking about infrastructure or hosting, which will be covered in the Physical Architecture section. The focus here is purely on the internal logical organization of the software: modules, services, features, and the relationships between them.

A few examples of what we are looking for: a product may have a core processing engine, a notification module, and a user management layer. We want to understand what each does and how they are connected. Or a complex feature may be the result of two or more components working in tandem — we want that relationship to be visible.

Technical depth is expected and welcome in this section.

**Post-LOI sub-questions.**
- 2.1 A description of the logical structure of the application: how it is organized (modules, services, features), what each component is responsible for, and how they interact with each other.
- 2.2 A description of the process used to create internal documentation. Internal documentation is about how the software works, how it's structured, how it can be deployed in different environments.
- 2.3 Access to the internal documentation (pdf, link, confluence, github).
- 2.4 A description of the process used to create customer-facing documentation (or user manual). This documentation is about how the user uses the software and how they expect it to work.
- 2.5 Access to the user manual.
- 2.6 A diagram of the logical architecture of the application, outlining its core building blocks and how they interact with each other (UML, draw.io, PNG, PDF).
- 2.7 A description of the database technology used by the application (SQL, NoSQL, key-value store, filesystem manager), including the rationale behind the choice, the features that benefit from it, and any features that may be constrained or weakened by it.
- 2.8 A description of how the application's services and modules communicate with each other, including the protocols and patterns used such as REST APIs, event-driven messaging, or batch processing.
- 2.9 A description of the application's known architectural limitations, including any planned redesigns on the roadmap and the rationale behind them.

