## 4. Tech Stack, Codebase & Engineering Team

**Scope.** This section focuses on the technical choices made to build the application and on how maintainable and extensible the codebase is in practice. Specifically, it covers the languages, frameworks, and tools that make up the tech stack, the structure and quality of the codebase, and the engineering team responsible for it. We are not yet asking about infrastructure or architecture, which have been covered in previous sections.

The focus here is on the day-to-day technical reality of the software: what it is built with, how easy it is to change, and who builds and maintains it.

**Post-LOI sub-questions.**
- 4.1 A description of the backend technology stack, covering the languages, frameworks, and libraries used across the different parts of the application, along with the rationale behind each of these technology choices.
- 4.2 A description of the frontend technology stack, covering the languages, frameworks, and libraries used across the different parts of the application, along with the rationale behind each of these technology choices.
- 4.3 A description of the application's codebase, including its age, how it is maintained over time, and whether an active test suite is in place.
- 4.4 A description of the application's known areas of technical debt.
- 4.5 A description of the process followed to introduce new features into the existing codebase.
- 4.6 Access to the application's code repository or repositories.
- 4.7 A description of the development environment, including how it is set up and used by the engineering team to build and test new features.
- 4.8 A description of the staging environment, including how it mirrors production and how it is used to validate changes before release.
- 4.9 A description of the CI/CD strategy and deployment process in place, including the steps and automation used to build, test, and promote changes from development through to production.
- 4.10 A description of the technical team composition, including the roles, skills, and seniority levels of the staff involved in developing and maintaining the application.
- 4.11 A description of the process used to allocate engineering staff to features/clients.

