## 7. Proprietary Data & Data Advantages

**Scope.** This section covers the data assets owned by the company, focusing on their uniqueness and the ease with which they can be leveraged. Specifically, it examines what proprietary data the company holds, how difficult it would be for a competitor to replicate it, and how readily it can be used to generate value.

A few examples of what we are looking for: whether the company has accumulated unique datasets that are hard to obtain elsewhere; how structured and accessible the data is for analysis or product development; and whether the data represents a meaningful competitive advantage.

**Post-LOI sub-questions.**
- 7.1 A description of the data assets the company holds, covering both structured and unstructured data, the current volume, and the rate at which new data is being generated.
- 7.2 A description of how the data is stored and accessed, including the tools, systems, and processes required to query and retrieve it.
- 7.3 A description of how data is collected and the extent to which the company is free to use it to implement AI-powered features, including any contractual or consent-based restrictions.
- 7.4 A description of how data is collected and the extent to which the company is free to use it to train machine learning models, including any rights, restrictions, or obligations tied to the data sources.

---

## 8. AI Models

**Scope.** This section covers the models used within the application (whether proprietary, foundational, or open-source), how each is used, and how they are hosted and served. Specifically, it examines the role each model plays in the application, the lifecycle of proprietary models from training to deployment, the degree of dependency on external model providers, and the costs and risks associated with each.

A few examples of what we are looking for: whether the company has developed its own models and what makes them defensible; which foundation models or external AI solutions are used and how easy it would be to switch providers; whether open-source models are self-hosted or externally served; and any past incidents related to model output quality or hallucinations, including their impact and resolution.

**Post-LOI sub-questions.**
- 8.1 A description of the OCR module, including whether it is built on a proprietary model or relies on an external provider, and how tightly the core application functionality depends on it.
- 8.2 A description of whether AI models are used elsewhere in the application, and if so, which functions they serve.
- 8.3 A description of how the company uses AI internally to support its own processes and operations, beyond what is exposed to end users in the product.
- 8.4 A description of the Azure Translator integration, including how it is used within the application, which features depend on it, and how tightly the core application functionality relies on it.
- 8.5 A description of the Azure OpenAI integration, including which models are used, how they are embedded in the application's features, and how tightly the core application functionality depends on it.
- 8.6 A description of the AI talent within the company, including the size and composition of the team, what they work on, and whether their focus is on building and maintaining proprietary models, integrating external AI solutions, or both.
